+# Specification Quality Checklist: Phone Redaction and Output Folder
+
+**Purpose**: Validate specification completeness and quality before proceeding to planning
+**Created**: 2026-11-21
+**Feature**: [spec.md](../spec.md)
+
+## Content Quality
+
+- [x] No implementation details (languages, frameworks, APIs)
+- [x] Focused on user value and business needs
+- [x] Written for non-technical stakeholders
+- [x] All mandatory sections completed
+
+## Requirement Completeness
+
+- [x] No [NEEDS CLARIFICATION] markers remain
+- [x] Requirements are testable and unambiguous
+- [x] Success criteria are measurable
+- [x] Success criteria are technology-agnostic (no implementation details)
+- [x] All acceptance scenarios are defined
+- [x] Edge cases are identified
+- [x] Scope is clearly bounded
+- [x] Dependencies and assumptions identified
+
+## Feature Readiness
+
+- [x] All functional requirements have clear acceptance criteria
+- [x] User scenarios cover primary flows
+- [x] Feature meets measurable outcomes defined in Success Criteria
+- [x] No implementation details leak into specification
+
+## Constitution Gates (R039 Constitution v1.0.0)
+
+- [x] **Principle V — Specification gate**: a Security Requirements section is present, derived against the OWASP Top 10 (2021) and ASVS 4.0, naming the categories considered and recording those judged not applicable with a reason (SR-001 - SR-012 and the OWASP table)
+- [x] **Principle III**: every functional and security requirement is stated as an observable assertion, and FR-019 requires test coverage including negative tests asserting both exit status and message content
+- [x] **Principle II**: every new failure path (missing `--out-dir` value, empty value, `--out-dir` with no FILE, undirectable output path, unwritable output, link or directory at the output path, output path equal to an input path) specifies a diagnostic plus a non-zero exit status, with no silent coercion (FR-012, FR-015, SR-005, SR-006, SR-008, SR-009, SR-012)
+- [x] **Principle I**: FR-018 keeps the runtime standard-library-only
+- [x] **Security Baseline — path containment**: SR-005 requires resolution and a direct-child check against the output folder; SR-006 forbids writing through a link or onto a non-regular file
+- [x] **Security Baseline — file permissions**: SR-007 requires `0o700` directories and `0o600` files, set at creation
+- [x] **Security Baseline — resource limits**: SR-011 carries the 1 MiB per-line bound into both destinations and bounds open output handles to one
+- [x] **Security Baseline — output hygiene**: SR-001 and SR-010 keep personal data out of diagnostics, the summary, the tally line, and every file on disk
+
+## Validation Notes
+
+### Iteration 1 — 2026-11-21
+
+Reviewed the drafted spec against every item above. Findings and resolutions:
+
+1. **Backward-compatibility claim needed to be a requirement, not prose.** The user's instruction "everything else it does now must keep working exactly as it does" was initially only context. It is now binding as FR-005 (behavioural identity for everything feature 001 defines) and FR-017 (no-`--out-dir` runs unchanged), is measurable as SC-003, and is tabulated requirement-by-requirement in "Relationship to feature 001". *Resolved.*
+
+2. **Phone/card overlap was the one genuinely ambiguous point.** The two rules overlap for a `+` followed by 13-15 Luhn-valid digits. Rather than leave a marker, the choice is forced by FR-005: phone takes rank 4 so no currently-redacted span changes category. Recorded in FR-003, in the Edge Cases, and with its rationale in Assumptions. *Resolved without a clarification marker.*
+
+3. **Edge-case claims verified against the existing implementation** rather than asserted. `+4222222222222` → `+[CARD]` (13 digits, Luhn-valid), `+10.0.0.7` → `+[IP]`, `+14155552671@example.com` → `[EMAIL]`, `user jane@example.com from 10.0.0.7 card 4111 1111 1111 1111` → `user [EMAIL] from [IP] card [CARD]`, and a 16-digit run left untouched were each confirmed to be today's behaviour, so the "unchanged" claims in the spec are factual. *Resolved.*
+
+4. **`--out-dir` with no FILE was unspecified by the description.** Both "write to a file named after standard input" and "fall back to standard output" were rejected under Principle II as substituting a value for an invocation that cannot be honoured. FR-012 makes it a usage error with nothing created. Rationale recorded in Assumptions. *Resolved.*
+
+5. **Three cases the description does not mention but a filesystem writer must answer** were added, each as a testable requirement: an output path that resolves outside the folder (SR-005), an output path occupied by a symbolic link or non-regular file (SR-006), and an output path that is the same file as an input FILE, e.g. `--out-dir .` (SR-008). Without these the feature would ship a path-traversal and an input-truncation defect. *Resolved.*
+
+6. **Singular/plural of the tally line.** The description shows only `wrote 2 files`. FR-010 fixes `wrote 1 file` for the single-file case, with the reason in Assumptions. *Resolved.*
+
+7. **Duplicate final path components** (`logs/a/app.log logs/b/app.log`) make "the number of output files written" ambiguous. FR-011 defines N as FILE arguments processed, with the reading recorded in Assumptions. *Resolved.*
+
+8. **Success criteria were checked for technology leakage.** SC-001 - SC-012 are stated as observable outcomes — values absent from output, bytes identical, files present with given permissions, diagnostics present with a given exit status, wall-clock budgets. No framework, module, or language construct is named in that section. *Pass.*
+
+9. **Implementation detail check on the body.** The spec names the user-facing surface (the `--out-dir` option, the `wrote N files` line, the four-field summary, exit status 2, the 1 MiB limit, `0o600`/`0o700`) because each is an externally observable contract the user or the constitution fixed. It names no module, function, regular expression, or data structure. `pytest` and Python 3.11 appear only where the constitution and feature 001 already mandate them as project constraints. *Pass.*
+
+**Result**: all items pass on iteration 1. No [NEEDS CLARIFICATION] markers remain; the eight points where the description was silent are resolved as documented assumptions, each individually revisitable via `/speckit-clarify`.
+
